# Public env vars (NEXT_PUBLIC_* are inlined into the build).
# Copy to .env.local for local testing; set in Cloudflare Pages → Settings →
# Variables and Secrets for production builds.

# GA4 measurement ID. Must match the format G-XXXXXXXXXX.
# Leave empty to disable GA4 entirely (component renders nothing).
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-0FCGXJHMMY

# Cookiebot Domain Group ID (UUID). Leave empty to disable the consent
# banner — useful for local dev when you don't want the banner in the way.
# NOTE: since 2026-08-08 the banner really does gate Google and Meta. Leaving
# this empty disables Cookiebot, which means the Consent Mode default in
# components/analytics/consent-default.tsx is never updated: GA4 then stays
# denied (no _ga cookie) and the Meta Pixel never loads. That is the intended
# fail-closed behaviour, not a misconfiguration.
NEXT_PUBLIC_COOKIEBOT_CBID=

# PostHog (product analytics, EU cloud). NEXT_PUBLIC_* → inlined into the
# client bundle (PostHog is browser-only here; static export has no server
# runtime). The key is a public client-side ingestion key.
#
# NO VALUE HERE ON PURPOSE, and this is a change from before. Since the
# 2026-08-12 platform cutover this site reports into the PLATFORM's PostHog
# project, not the old shared project 143695 (which is left to BreedSelector).
# The real value lives ONLY in the Cloudflare Pages dashboard, scoped to
# Production and Preview — a committed key here would go stale silently the
# next time the project moves, and this file is documentation the build never
# reads. Ask Jur, or read it from the Pages variables.
#
# Two traps: NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined AT BUILD TIME, so changing the variable
# does nothing until a build runs; and an empty or wrong key fails silent — the
# provider just skips init and the site measures nothing, with no error anywhere.
# Leave KEY empty to disable PostHog (provider renders children, skips init).
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com

# Meta (Facebook) Pixel id — 15-16 digits. Public by nature (it ships in the
# page source), like the GA4 id above. Leave empty to disable the pixel
# entirely (the component renders nothing).
# MUST be set in Cloudflare Pages -> Settings -> Variables and Secrets for BOTH
# Production AND Preview as a PLAINTEXT variable — NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at
# build time, so an unset variable means the pixel silently never renders.
# Does not load until Cookiebot grants marketing consent, and stops on
# withdrawal (see the header comment in components/analytics/meta-pixel.tsx).
#
# Dataset "Letsdog A team", in use since 2026-08-11 (platform loop decision
# D-99). The platform sends Purchase into this SAME dataset over the Conversions
# API, so this value has to stay in step with it: a funnel split across two
# datasets reports no purchases at all, on either one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=958837033882897

# Cloudflare Pages reads NODE_VERSION from env to pin the build container's
# Node version. Set to 20 in Pages production env, not committed here.
# NODE_VERSION=20

# --- Contact + creator form (posts to the platform since 2026-09-14, T-83) ---
# The two forms POST straight from the browser to the platform's
# submit-website-contact (lib/website-contact.ts); there is no server code in this
# repo any more. The Turnstile SECRET for this site's widget lives on the platform
# as WEBSITE_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY. So POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN, CONTACT_TO,
# CONTACT_FROM, CREATORS_TO and TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY are no longer read by
# anything here and can be deleted from the Cloudflare dashboard.
#
# What stays here is the public half of the widget. Create a Managed widget in
# the Cloudflare dashboard with hostnames letsdog.nl + www.letsdog.nl +
# website-letsdog.pages.dev. In Pages -> Variables and Secrets, PRODUCTION scope:
#   - NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY = the public Site Key, as a PLAINTEXT variable
#     (must be inlined into the client bundle at build; do NOT store it as a Secret).
# Leave the Preview scope UNSET: branch previews fall back to Cloudflare's
# always-pass TEST key, whose token the platform's real secret refuses — the form
# renders on a preview but cannot deliver from one (by design).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# --- Error sink (Sentry, T-44 / loop decision D-6) ---
# Read by the browser half (lib/error-sink.ts), inlined into the client bundle by
# `next build`. (Until 2026-09-14 the contact Pages Function read the same
# variable from `env` and reported into the same project under
# `runtime: pages-function`; that Function was retired with T-83.)
#
# Project: EU ingest, name `website`. Set it in Cloudflare Pages -> Settings ->
# Variables and Secrets as a PLAINTEXT variable, scoped to BOTH Production and
# Preview. A DSN is public by design — it ships in the client bundle either
# way — but it still lives only in the dashboard, never in this repo, and the
# value below is deliberately NOT the real one.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED STATE, not a misconfiguration: the sink then reports
# nothing and breaks nothing.
# Two traps, the same two as every other NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable here: it is
# inlined AT BUILD TIME, so setting it changes nothing until a build runs; and
# a malformed value fails silent (parseSentryDsn returns null -> no-op).
#
# What is reported: the handover-cookie contract breaches, the forms' round trip
# to the platform failing (no answer, a refusal we caused, a captcha refusal),
# and a consent chain that never started.
# Deliberately NOT: all unhandled browser errors (D-6).
# What NEVER travels: the visitor's consent choice. That is enforced by an
# allowlist in lib/error-report.ts, not by call-site discipline.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
